            <ArticleTitle>Spontaneous mediastinal hemorrhage. A complication of intracoronary streptokinase infusion for coronary thrombosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>562-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 72-year-old man with a recent inferior-wall myocardial infarction complicated by continued ischemic cardiac pain underwent an intracoronary streptokinase infusion in an attempt to re-establish coronary perfusion. Although the cardiac catheterization and streptokinase infusion were technically uncomplicated, signs of an enlarging mediastinal mass associated with a drop in the hematocrit reading developed in the patient 12 hours after the procedure. Aortography showed no evidence of aortic dissection or laceration. The patient was believed to have a spontaneous mediastinal hemorrhage related to streptokinase infusion.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
